1
00:00:10,430 --> 00:00:15,045
Let's get started.
We've gone through

2
00:00:15,045 --> 00:00:20,610
running BIRD in a topology
using container lab.

3
00:00:20,610 --> 00:00:23,340
Now in this lesson,

4
00:00:23,340 --> 00:00:28,270
I just want to talk about
some other resources that are

5
00:00:28,270 --> 00:00:31,110
available that might
help you create

6
00:00:31,110 --> 00:00:36,210
larger router experiments
and things like that.

7
00:00:36,840 --> 00:00:41,190
A couple of the key questions
here would be like,

8
00:00:41,190 --> 00:00:45,590
how do we get real routing
tables and announcements?

9
00:00:45,590 --> 00:00:47,880
In our little walk through,

10
00:00:47,880 --> 00:00:52,970
we used just some static
routes that we set in BIRD,

11
00:00:52,970 --> 00:00:55,570
we saw those get announced.

12
00:00:55,570 --> 00:01:00,020
But how do we get some realness
to some routing tables,

13
00:01:00,020 --> 00:01:02,350
some announcements
and inject those.

14
00:01:02,350 --> 00:01:04,210
How can we use those to inject

15
00:01:04,210 --> 00:01:07,840
those announcements in
a programmatic manner?

16
00:01:07,840 --> 00:01:11,685
Container Lab is nice
but it's pretty manual.

17
00:01:11,685 --> 00:01:14,930
Namely they'll set up
a BIRD and all that.

18
00:01:14,930 --> 00:01:18,550
Can we get something with
richer capabilities?

19
00:01:18,770 --> 00:01:22,140
There's resources
for each of these,

20
00:01:22,140 --> 00:01:26,890
so there's RIPE which runs

21
00:01:26,890 --> 00:01:32,030
a routing data collector
to get injected.

22
00:01:32,030 --> 00:01:34,735
There's a tool called ExaBGP.

23
00:01:34,735 --> 00:01:37,230
Then with richer capabilities

24
00:01:37,230 --> 00:01:39,355
we have the SEED
Internet emulator.

25
00:01:39,355 --> 00:01:41,125
Let me walk through each.

26
00:01:41,125 --> 00:01:46,850
RIPE is a regional Internet
registry for Europe,

27
00:01:46,850 --> 00:01:51,725
the Middle East, parts
of Central Asia.

28
00:01:51,725 --> 00:01:56,175
They run a routing data
collection platform,

29
00:01:56,175 --> 00:01:58,695
so that's basically
they're running routers.

30
00:01:58,695 --> 00:02:03,390
They have them peered to other
routers on the Internet,

31
00:02:03,390 --> 00:02:07,110
and they're actually
collecting and storing all of

32
00:02:07,110 --> 00:02:09,670
the route announcements
that they get and

33
00:02:09,670 --> 00:02:13,630
then taking snapshots of
those routing tables.

34
00:02:13,820 --> 00:02:18,450
Here's an example of
routing table dump.

35
00:02:18,450 --> 00:02:26,520
If you go to this
particular date in 2023,

36
00:02:26,520 --> 00:02:32,010
you can get a routing
table dump from that.

37
00:02:32,010 --> 00:02:36,570
It's stored in a
format called MRT,

38
00:02:36,570 --> 00:02:38,685
you can search for it.

39
00:02:38,685 --> 00:02:42,120
It's an open standard for it,

40
00:02:42,120 --> 00:02:45,190
and there are some
utilities that they

41
00:02:45,190 --> 00:02:50,145
provide that can read
these different formats,

42
00:02:50,145 --> 00:02:54,960
and they provide a
container docker image

43
00:02:54,960 --> 00:02:56,910
that has this installed,

44
00:02:56,910 --> 00:02:58,485
and here's an example,

45
00:02:58,485 --> 00:03:03,990
if you unzip that you can run

46
00:03:03,990 --> 00:03:07,760
this command bgpdump
with some of

47
00:03:07,760 --> 00:03:09,560
these things and just provide

48
00:03:09,560 --> 00:03:12,540
what you just downloaded
and then unzipped.

49
00:03:12,540 --> 00:03:15,840
It will have lines like this.

50
00:03:15,840 --> 00:03:17,980
This is one line in

51
00:03:17,980 --> 00:03:20,555
that it's the dump of
the routing table.

52
00:03:20,555 --> 00:03:25,440
Here, it'll have the
prefix and the AS path.

53
00:03:25,440 --> 00:03:27,965
We saw this in our routers.

54
00:03:27,965 --> 00:03:30,240
But now we can say,

55
00:03:30,240 --> 00:03:32,240
this is a real Internet router

56
00:03:32,240 --> 00:03:34,460
and we can get a real
Internet routing table,

57
00:03:34,460 --> 00:03:38,400
and then it will be
the correct size.

58
00:03:39,290 --> 00:03:41,980
Then how do you use that?

59
00:03:41,980 --> 00:03:47,530
One possible tool to
use is called ExaBGP.

60
00:03:47,530 --> 00:03:52,540
ExaBGP is cool in that
it's a BGP agent,

61
00:03:52,540 --> 00:03:54,440
but it's really just
meant for testing.

62
00:03:54,440 --> 00:03:58,960
It has a degree of
programmability in it.

63
00:03:59,030 --> 00:04:03,330
You can add these routes
in the configuration,

64
00:04:03,330 --> 00:04:07,095
and what's cool is you can
add the actual AS paths.

65
00:04:07,095 --> 00:04:09,600
Let's say we had a MRT,

66
00:04:09,600 --> 00:04:12,300
a line from that
routing table dump

67
00:04:12,300 --> 00:04:16,025
that had a particular
prefix and an AS path.

68
00:04:16,025 --> 00:04:19,900
This is not something I can
add to a BIRD configuration,

69
00:04:19,900 --> 00:04:25,220
but it's something that I can
add to an ExaBGP process.

70
00:04:25,220 --> 00:04:30,205
What it will do is it will
announce this to its peers.

71
00:04:30,205 --> 00:04:37,350
It will announce that
route with this AS path,

72
00:04:37,350 --> 00:04:39,555
and then it'll add on its own.

73
00:04:39,555 --> 00:04:41,890
Then it looks like
to the neighbor,

74
00:04:41,890 --> 00:04:44,370
oh, this is multiple hops away.

75
00:04:44,370 --> 00:04:48,570
This allows us to
basically take a corner of

76
00:04:48,570 --> 00:04:50,790
the Internet and then just

77
00:04:50,790 --> 00:04:54,800
emulate that maybe there's
a handful of routers there,

78
00:04:54,800 --> 00:04:57,610
and then we have the routes
from what the rest of

79
00:04:57,610 --> 00:05:02,340
the Internet would look like.

80
00:05:02,340 --> 00:05:06,870
So there is a Python
programmable, BGP agent.

81
00:05:06,870 --> 00:05:12,215
You can programatically
do some announcements.

82
00:05:12,215 --> 00:05:16,055
This can be useful,
like if I want to say,

83
00:05:16,055 --> 00:05:18,305
announce something, withdraw it,

84
00:05:18,305 --> 00:05:21,340
whatever I'm trying to test out.

85
00:05:21,340 --> 00:05:27,280
I can create some Python program
to do that using ExaBGP.

86
00:05:27,850 --> 00:05:30,930
The last tool that I'm
going to mention is

87
00:05:30,930 --> 00:05:34,060
called the SEED
Internet emulator.

88
00:05:34,060 --> 00:05:39,725
This is a project that came
out of Syracuse University,

89
00:05:39,725 --> 00:05:44,410
some academics, and it's been
around for several years,

90
00:05:44,410 --> 00:05:46,390
but it's a really
powerful thing.

91
00:05:46,390 --> 00:05:49,450
I would highly encourage you
to look at their slides,

92
00:05:49,450 --> 00:05:53,410
look at their Github,
is freely available.

93
00:05:53,410 --> 00:05:57,065
These slides are from them,
just to introduce it.

94
00:05:57,065 --> 00:06:02,045
The design of it provides
a bunch of primitives.

95
00:06:02,045 --> 00:06:05,420
Instead of just a container
and then you can run bird.

96
00:06:05,420 --> 00:06:10,730
It provides some
different abstractions

97
00:06:10,730 --> 00:06:15,245
like I can run ton system
or an Internet exchange,

98
00:06:15,245 --> 00:06:20,705
or group these as a network
or just run a router,

99
00:06:20,705 --> 00:06:25,415
create a host to service all
these different primitives.

100
00:06:25,415 --> 00:06:33,485
You specify their topology
in a Python program.

101
00:06:33,485 --> 00:06:36,365
Then it will go
and compose that.

102
00:06:36,365 --> 00:06:42,065
It will leverage docker and
you can also use it across,

103
00:06:42,065 --> 00:06:45,935
it works beyond
just a single node.

104
00:06:45,935 --> 00:06:48,920
You can run it on
multiple nodes.

105
00:06:48,920 --> 00:06:51,320
Take each part of
the topology so it

106
00:06:51,320 --> 00:06:53,765
